# Line endings.
#
# Without a rule here, whatever line endings a file happened to be committed
# with are what git stores forever, and the repo drifted: 167 of ~1450 text
# files were stored CRLF (Windows-side edits) and the rest LF. Any tool that
# reads-and-rewrites a file — most editors, and anything using Python's
# text-mode `write_text` — silently converts, turning a two-line change into a
# whole-file diff. That happened three times in one day on care.py,
# frontend/package.json and test_db_seam.py.
#
# So: the repository stores LF for every text file. `text=auto` lets git decide
# what counts as text; `eol=lf` is what lands in the working tree on checkout,
# on Windows too. Nothing in the toolchain (node, python, vite, pytest) cares,
# and every editor Leon uses handles LF.
#
# Committed alongside a one-time `git add --renormalize .` — without it the
# working tree and the stored blobs disagree and every one of those 167 files
# shows as modified.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF — CRLF breaks them under bash/WSL ("bad interpreter: ...^M").
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-side scripts, run by PowerShell/cmd on Leon's desktop. These are the
# one place CRLF is the correct answer, so they are pinned rather than left to
# the rule above.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touch, never diff as text. git's own detection would catch
# these, but being explicit means a stray 0x0D in an .avif can't be mistaken
# for a line ending.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.mp4  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.npy  binary
